            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue.\r\n\r\nThis is [kind of expected](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/654c723046d8f06151c7e940819c66030df011e7/doc/mini-animate.txt#L38-L43) since 'scroll' animation directly affects Neovim state (window view in this case). So what actually happens is that `vim.diagnostic.goto_next()` is executed and window is shown (or scheduled to be shown, not sure) but 'mini.animate' moves view back to the beginning and starts animation. The latter closes diagnostic window and it is not opened again.\r\n\r\nThe [suggested approach](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/654c723046d8f06151c7e940819c66030df011e7/doc/mini-animate.txt#L285-L291) to mitigate this is to use [`execute_after()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/654c723046d8f06151c7e940819c66030df011e7/doc/mini-animate.txt#L456). Something like this:\r\n```lua\r\n_G.diagnostic_goto = function(direction)\r\n  vim.diagnostic['goto_' .. direction]()\r\n  MiniAnimate.execute_after('scroll', function() vim.defer_fn(vim.diagnostic.open_float, 5) end)\r\nend\r\n```\r\nThen execute like `lua _G.diagnostic_goto('next')`.\r\n\r\nClosing as a documented issue with workaround.",
            "created_at": "2023-08-13T18:09:10Z",
